From 26f5b62585d0c5b946bfd8662688fdcfafce591f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 21:40:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09108/11362] Record the ARC held-out baseline: 8 of 25 clear,
 and sc25 stops after 24 actions

The first full sweep of REQ-ARC-BENCH-6267. This is the number the ARC loop now
steers on, replacing `reproducible_total_levels`, which is pinned at 183 of 183.

25 games, adapters bypassed, 3000 expansions, 5.5 minutes, zero errors:

  8 of 25 clear at least one level   (clear rate 0.32)
  364,730 actions spent
  45,591 actions per level cleared

The clear rate is what makes this usable. Not zero, not saturated, so a change is
readable in both directions and 17 level-0 games are the improvement surface.
Spend spans 232 actions (vc33, cleared) to 74,700 (g50t, not cleared), so the
efficiency half discriminates too.

FOUND ON THE FIRST RUN: sc25 spends 24 actions and stops. Every other level-0
game spends between 8,155 and 74,700 -- sc25 is roughly 300x below the next
cheapest failure. That is not a hard game, that is exploration terminating
almost immediately, and it matters more than one row: a game the search refuses
to explore is invisible to every flag measurement that follows, because no flag
can change an outcome decided before the search runs. Filed in known-issues for
an investigation slot rather than chased here.

Numbers are public games with the hand-written adapter bypassed, which removes
the route and not the knowledge that produced it. Not a hidden-game result.
